cells with very low levels of mrna  base 2 they're still getting infected  very well  indicating that actually the mrna level  of h2 is not  fully correlated correlated with the  protein levels of ace2 in these tissues  and  um in data that i'm not showing today we  also look at  how  h2 levels in the mice brain are  somewhere in the range of like 10 000  fold lower than the lungs however  these human h2 knock-in mice  tend to succumb to brain infections and  not lung infections  indicating that ace2 really acts as a  gateway of  the virus entering the cell of interest  but does not necessarily  mean like high levels higher levels  equal doesn't mean it's more susceptible  to the virus  um along those lines and testing the h2  requirement we we also wanted to make  sure on the other end that sars code v2  required um  the spike to bind to the h2 to infect  these  brain organoids we are very lucky um to  get uh to have a collaborator who  provided a spinal uh css from patients  with colvin 19.  and then we were we were able to show  that the csf in these patients actually  had  neutralizing antibodies against  the  sarcob2  and with that we did a similar essay  where we used the human brain organoid  and co-incubated the virus with  the csf in order to see if the  antibodies in the csf can truly prevent  infection of  the organoids  and what we saw was similar to blocking  ace2  that the csf antibodies were sufficient  in blocking infection of neural tissues  and with that we were able to kind of  definitively show that saris cov2 can  infect neural tissues through the route  of ace2  with the csf that we were able to obtain  we were able to do some additional  experiments and this is in a in a  separate manuscript that chris bartley  at ucsf and i worked on  and  one of the things that  really kind of  was very exciting to us was  by using saris cov2 almost as a model we  were able to kind of think about  other  effects that viral infections can have  on the central nervous system  um in in in human biology and in a more  kind of broad setting  so one of the things that we we noticed  was that  coven 19 uh resulted in  neutralizing or blocking antibodies  against spike  in both the csf shown on the left and in  the plasma of same patients  but what we noticed uh was  while they all  were able to produce this anti-spike  antibody  the epitopes that were present in the  csf and then the plasma were very unique  to each compartment  so you can see here the s552589  epitope is very highly expressed in the  csf but much more lower in the plasma  while  the  818855 epitope is low in the csf but  high in in the plasma indicating that  you know the two compartments are  actually immunologically unique  um and this was actually shown in uh  more recent papers by the kelowna and  you know the kidness groups um looking  at kind of  uh  bone marrow like skull bone marrow and  meninges uh native b cells and  neutrophils  but also you know the immune responses  that we we imagine that's occurring in  the csf versus the plasma really  are unique to each other and we have to  think about the two compartments as a as  a separate entity  and in order to kind of dive into this a  little bit more we we were able to  create a new mouse model of stars cov2  using human ace ii over expression using  an adeno-associated virus and that can  be checked out by a paper a postdoc in  our lab then israelo and i wrote  last year  and by using this adopting this model we  were able to  localize the infection the sarcoidominal  infection and very specific compartments  of the mouth so either just the lung as  shown in the top here in both the lung  and the brain shown in the middle here  or just in the brain  and with this we were able to answer  kind of this  you know long mysterious question about  how can we use csf to understand what's  going on in the central nervous system  so  what we did was we infected um these  mice that were only expressing you know  human h2 in the lung only in the brain  or both the lung in the brain and then  tracked antibody responses in each  compartment  so first when we only infect it in the  lung  we we expect to see the antibodies um  you know be produced only in the lung  and the serum right and then the brain  and the csf there's very little antibody  responses  which is very expected  when we look at  infection that's occurring in both the  lung and the brain we now see antibodies  at high levels in all four compartments  including lung serum brain and csf shown  here at yellow  finally when we isolated the infection  only into the brain what we saw was that  the lung and the serum actually had  significantly lower amounts of antibody  being produced i mean actually the brain  and csf antibodies were very sensitive  to detecting  that infection in the brain was  occurring  and to kind of look at this even in more  detail we also checked viral titers  during this time  of the different compartments and in  mice that were infected in both the lung  and the brain  we were able to find high levels of the  virus  in the brain tissue and the lung tissue  itself  but we're unable to detect high levels  of the virus by qpcr in the csf or the  serum  and i think this is especially important  in thinking about kind of clinical  relevance of how we  tend to think about viral infections in  the cns  for many many  you know reasons one being access to  brain tissue being very difficult we use  csf as a surrogate for understanding  what's going in the brain  that also means many assays and many you  know diagnostic tools are designed to  detect and uh virus levels right to pcr  or whatever it may be in the css however  as shown here  that sometimes is not sensitive enough  or it's not the right kind of essay to  be able to definitively say a  neuro invasion occurred  and rather  being able to think about it with  antibodies might be more important  in making clinical diagnoses  um and then finally um today i don't  have time to go over this but i want to  give you guys a a peek at what chris  really contributed to  this project is  we were also able to show that in many  of these patients auto antibodies  against  cells in the brain  occurred in the csf of these copin 19  patients  and  chris did beautiful work using a  combination of mass spec  their  ip pull down strategies and mouse  validation um to show what the specific  target of all these auto antibodies  might be  indicating that in addition to the  response  effective immune response against covet  that occurs in these infections there's  also a dysfunctional dysregulated  autoimmune response that can occur um  giving us kind of an insight to  possibilities of why long covet might  occur  so with that  i just want to thank everyone again  especially  who  you know allowed us to use his beautiful  organoids for majority of the project  and chris who helped with the css story  and making that such an interesting and  fun project and collaboration to work  with  and of course akiko for allowing me to  perform all these experiments in their  lab and just always encouraging me  to chase any questions that i have  so thank you for your time and  i'll take in questions
 

